==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-}}
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|infect}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|infectus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|inficiō||dye, taint}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈfɛkt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-infect.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkt|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring (the body or part of it) into contact with a substance that causes illness (a [[pathogen]]), so that the pathogen begins to act on the body; (of a pathogen) to come into contact with (a body or body part) and begin to act on it. 
#: {{ux|en|Not everyone will be '''infected''' when an epidemic strikes.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=May-June|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/katie-l-burke Katie L. Burke]
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/3/in-the-news-45 In the News]
|volume=101|issue=3|page=193|magazine={{w|American Scientist}}
|passage=Bats host many high-profile viruses that can '''infect''' humans, including severe acute respiratory syndrome and Ebola.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To contaminate (an object or substance) with a pathogen. 
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make somebody enthusiastic about one's own passion, or to communicate a feeling to others, or a feeling communicating itself to others.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1834|author={{w|Letitia Elizabeth Landon}}|title=Francesca Carrara|volume=2|page=164|text=Guido, by way of diverting the embarrassment which seemed to '''infect''' them all, began to unfasten the packet of letters.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her passion for dancing has '''infected''' me.}}

